 1.1 Set $A=\omega_i(p)$ and $B=\omega_j(p)$. The integral equation $U(t)=I-\int_0^t\omega(\dot\gamma)U$ gives the positive-$i$ segment expansion $S_1=I-\varepsilon A+\tfrac12\varepsilon^2(A^2-\partial_iA)+O(\varepsilon^3)$. On the following positive-$j$ segment, Taylor expansion at $p+\varepsilon e_i$ gives $S_2=I-\varepsilon B+\varepsilon^2(\tfrac12B^2-\partial_iB-\tfrac12\partial_jB)+O(\varepsilon^3)$. In each case the second iterated integral contributes $\tfrac12\varepsilon^2\omega_k(p)^2$, and the first integral contributes the displayed derivative terms. [given, F1, algebra]
 
-1.2 Along the negative-$i$ segment from $p+\varepsilon e_i+\varepsilon e_j$ to $p+\varepsilon e_j$ and the negative-$j$ segment from $p+\varepsilon e_j$ to $p$, the same integral expansion gives $S_3=I+\varepsilon A+\varepsilon^2(\tfrac12A^2+\tfrac12\partial_iA+\partial_jA)+O(\varepsilon^3)$ and $S_4=I+\varepsilon B+\tfrac12\varepsilon^2(B^2+\partial_jB)+O(\varepsilon^3)$. The derivative terms use starting points $p+\varepsilon e_j$ and $p$; reversing the tangent changes the first-order ODE coefficient's sign. [given, F1, step 1.1, algebra]
+2.1 Along the negative-$i$ segment from $p+\varepsilon e_i+\varepsilon e_j$ to $p+\varepsilon e_j$ and the negative-$j$ segment from $p+\varepsilon e_j$ to $p$, the same integral expansion gives $S_3=I+\varepsilon A+\varepsilon^2(\tfrac12A^2+\tfrac12\partial_iA+\partial_jA)+O(\varepsilon^3)$ and $S_4=I+\varepsilon B+\tfrac12\varepsilon^2(B^2+\partial_jB)+O(\varepsilon^3)$. The derivative terms use starting points $p+\varepsilon e_j$ and $p$; reversing the tangent changes the first-order ODE coefficient's sign. [given, F1, step 1.1, algebra]
 
-2.1 Later segments multiply on the left, so total transport is $S_4S_3S_2S_1$. Its linear terms cancel. The within-segment quadratic terms sum to $A^2+B^2-\partial_iB+\partial_jA$; the six cross-products sum to $BA-B^2-BA-AB-A^2+BA=BA-AB-A^2-B^2$. The full coefficient is therefore $\partial_jA-\partial_iB+BA-AB=-(\partial_i\omega_j-\partial_j\omega_i+[A,B])=-F_{ij}(p)$ by [F2]. For a uniform remainder, choose a compact neighborhood $K'$ of $K$ contained in $V$ and containing the four square sides for $0<\varepsilon<\varepsilon_0$; the $C^2$ coefficients and derivatives are bounded there. Taylor remainders in single integrals are uniformly $O(\varepsilon^3)$, and if $M$ bounds coefficient norms, iterated-integral terms of order $m\ge3$ are bounded by $M^m\varepsilon^m/m!$, whose sum is $O(\varepsilon^3)$. Multiplying four expansions preserves the uniform remainder order. [given, F1, F2, step 1.1, step 1.2, algebra] ∎
